@startuml D2_PartB_MCP_Flow
' ============================================================
' DOMAIN 2 — PART B: MCP (Topics 12–26)
' FULL FLOW: connect -> discover -> route -> execute, across
' the standard MCP layer. Plug-and-play, model-agnostic.
' CORE TRAP: Claude REQUESTS; the CLIENT (your app) executes.
' The SERVER holds the credentials & talks to the backend.
' ============================================================
title Domain 2 · Part B — MCP (Topics 12–26)\n(One standard interface: connect -> discover -> route -> execute)

skinparam defaultFontSize 13
skinparam roundcorner 12
skinparam activityDiamondBackgroundColor #FFF6CC

start

' ---------- TOPIC 12: WHAT MCP IS / WHY ----------
partition "T12 · What MCP IS & WHY" {
  :Problem: N systems x custom integrations = a mess;
  #LightCyan:MCP = open, standard interface both sides agree on;
  note right
    == WHY (the real win) ==
    NOT more powerful than HTTP — it RUNS on HTTP.
    Win = STANDARDIZATION + DISCOVERY = PLUG-AND-PLAY.
    * Before: read N API docs, hand-write N integrations
      + N sets of tool defs, keep them in sync.
    * After: every server answers "what tools do you have?"
      in ONE format. Add a server = a CONFIG entry, not a sprint.
    MODEL-AGNOSTIC: server doesn't know/care who's on the
    other end -> write once, plug in anywhere (USB analogy).
    ----
    WHY USED : N systems x M clients = custom integrations explode.
    SOLVES   : write ONE server; every MCP client can use it.
    EXAMPLE  : one Jira server works in Claude Desktop, your app,
               an editor — no per-client rewrite.
    WHO CALLS: it's a PROTOCOL (a standard), not a caller — clients
               and servers both speak it; not a transport of its own.
  end note
}

' ---------- ROLES (Domain 1 lifecycle, restated) ----------
partition "The 3 ROLES (who does what)" {
  :Your APP = the MCP CLIENT;
  note right
    == THE ROUNDTRIP (same shape as D1) ==
    1. CLIENT connects to SERVER, calls list-tools
    2. CLIENT passes tool DEFINITIONS to Claude
    3. Claude REQUESTS a call (tool_use) — runs NOTHING
    4. CLIENT executes call-tool against the SERVER
    5. SERVER holds creds -> hits DB/API -> returns result
    6. CLIENT returns result to Claude -> final answer
    Claude never touches the server or the database directly.
    ----
    WHY USED : keep a clean split of who does what.
    SOLVES   : the "who executes?" confusion — Claude only REQUESTS.
    EXAMPLE  : Claude emits book_room(args) -> CLIENT runs it ->
               SERVER (holds creds) hits the booking API -> back.
    WHO CALLS: MODEL proposes; CLIENT (your app) executes;
               SERVER holds credentials + touches the backend.
  end note
}

' ---------- TOPIC 13: THREE PRIMITIVES ----------
partition "T13 · The 3 PRIMITIVES (who CONTROLS each)" {
  :TOOLS      — MODEL-controlled (Claude decides to call);
  :RESOURCES  — APP-controlled (app injects as context);
  :PROMPTS    — USER-controlled (human deliberately invokes);
  note right
    Memorize the 3-way OWNERSHIP split — exam's favorite angle.
    Ask: "WHO decides when this is used?"
    * Claude chooses  -> TOOL
    * App injects read-only -> RESOURCE
    * Human picks a template -> PROMPT
    ----
    WHY USED : one server exposes 3 KINDS of capability.
    SOLVES   : deciding how a capability should be offered + who
               is allowed to trigger it.
    EXAMPLE  : Jira -> TOOL create_ticket (Claude fires it),
               RESOURCE project_list (app injects), PROMPT
               "triage this bug" (human invokes).
    WHO CALLS: TOOL=model · RESOURCE=app · PROMPT=user.
  end note
}

' ---------- TOPICS 14–17: BUILDING & EXPOSING ----------
partition "T14–T17 · BUILD & EXPOSE" {
  :T14 · Build server (Python SDK)\ndocstring => description, type hints => schema;
  note right
    Part A still applies: a lazy DOCSTRING = a routing failure.
    ----
    WHY USED : you need to actually expose your system as a server.
    SOLVES   : turning functions into discoverable MCP tools.
    EXAMPLE  : @tool def get_orders(id:str)->... ; docstring becomes
               the routing description, type hints become the schema.
    WHO CALLS: YOU (developer) author the server.
  end note

  :T15 · Expose TOOLS: list-tools (discover) + call-tool (execute);
  note right
    Order: CONNECT -> DISCOVER (list) -> CHOOSE (Claude) -> CALL.
    Validation + useful errors (T6) live IN the server.
    ----
    WHY USED : a client must first learn what exists, then act.
    SOLVES   : discovery (list) vs execution (call) as two steps.
    EXAMPLE  : list-tools -> [get_orders, refund] ; Claude picks ->
               call-tool refund(order_id).
    WHO CALLS: CLIENT calls list/call; MODEL chooses between them.
  end note

  :T16 · Expose RESOURCES (read-only, each has a URI);
  note right
    A TOOL *does* something; a RESOURCE *is* something.
    Resources arrive AS CONTEXT (given); Claude just reads.
    Claude does NOT pick resources — the APP does.
    Want Claude to choose? Wrap it as a TOOL instead.
    ----
    WHY USED : some things are DATA to read, not actions to run.
    SOLVES   : giving context WITHOUT letting the model go fetch it.
    EXAMPLE  : a config file, DB schema, ticket body — exposed by URI.
    WHO CALLS: APP decides to inject it; Claude only READS it.
  end note

  :T17 · PROMPT templates (server-authored, parameterized);
  note right
    Server AUTHORS the recipe; USER invokes it + supplies params;
    app is the messenger. Packages expertise / team consistency.
    Prompts = the ONLY primitive that waits for a HUMAN.
    ----
    WHY USED : package a repeatable workflow the team invokes.
    SOLVES   : consistency — everyone runs the same vetted recipe.
    EXAMPLE  : "/triage-bug" template surfaced as a slash command.
    WHO CALLS: the USER deliberately picks it (+ supplies params).
  end note
}

' ---------- TOPICS 18–20: CLIENTS & TRANSPORTS ----------
partition "T18–T20 · CLIENTS & TRANSPORTS" {
  :T18 · Client lifecycle: connect -> HANDSHAKE -> list-tools -> loop;
  note right
    HANDSHAKE = both declare capabilities + protocol VERSION.
    Mismatches caught HERE, at connect time — not at runtime.
    ----
    WHY USED : both sides must agree before any work happens.
    SOLVES   : version/capability mismatches surfacing early.
    EXAMPLE  : client+server exchange protocol version at connect.
    WHO CALLS: CLIENT initiates connect + handshake to the SERVER.
  end note

  if (Where does the server run?) then (LOCAL)
    #LightYellow:T19 · STDIO transport;
    note right
      Client spawns server as a SUBPROCESS on same machine.
      Talk over stdin/stdout — no network, no ports, no auth.
      Naturally secure. 1 client per server, same box only.
      (How Claude Desktop runs local servers.)
      ----
      WHY USED : local server, same machine, no network needed.
      SOLVES   : simple secure local wiring (no ports/auth).
      EXAMPLE  : Claude Desktop spawns a local filesystem server.
      WHO CALLS: CLIENT spawns the SERVER as a subprocess.
    end note
  else (REMOTE / PROD)
    #LightBlue:T20 · Streamable HTTP transport;
    note right
      Server runs elsewhere; client talks over HTTP.
      Many clients, real AUTH, scales horizontally.
      "Streamable" = long-lived connection (Server-Sent Events)
      so server can PUSH progress mid-task.
      ----
      WHY USED : remote server serving many clients in production.
      SOLVES   : scale + real auth + long-running progress push.
      EXAMPLE  : a hosted company server behind OAuth, many users.
      WHO CALLS: CLIENT connects over HTTP to a remote SERVER.
    end note
  endif
}

' ---------- TOPICS 21–22: ADVANCED PROTOCOL ----------
partition "T21–T22 · Advanced protocol features" {
  #Plum:T21 · SAMPLING (the inversion);
  note right
    Normally client asks model. SAMPLING = SERVER asks the model,
    THROUGH the client. Server (e.g. log-summarizer) needs no key:
    it says "run this prompt" -> CLIENT makes the LLM call with
    ITS key -> returns text. Client stays in control (approve/log);
    creds never leave your side. Server keeps working with the result.
    ----
    WHY USED : a server needs an LLM but shouldn't hold a key.
    SOLVES   : lets servers use the model without their own creds.
    EXAMPLE  : log-summarizer server says "run this prompt" ->
               client's model answers -> server uses the text.
    WHO CALLS: SERVER requests; CLIENT makes the actual LLM call
               (with its key) and can approve/log it.
  end note

  :T22 · NOTIFICATIONS · ROOTS · PROGRESS;
  note right
    * NOTIFICATIONS — server tells client "something changed"
      (e.g. tool list) so client refreshes vs polling.
    * ROOTS — client tells server which DIRECTORIES it may touch
      (a boundary; file server can't wander outside).
    * PROGRESS — the % -done stream for long jobs (needs streaming).
    ----
    WHY USED : keep client + server in sync during a live session.
    SOLVES   : stale tool lists, unbounded file access, silent waits.
    EXAMPLE  : ROOTS limits a file server to /project only.
    WHO CALLS: NOTIFY+PROGRESS = server->client; ROOTS = client->server.
  end note
}

' ---------- TOPICS 23–24: SCOPING & PRODUCTION ----------
partition "T23–T24 · SCOPING & PRODUCTION" {
  #Yellow:T23 · BOUNDARIES · SCOPING · LEAST PRIVILEGE;
  note right
    D1 least-privilege at the SERVER level: expose the NARROWEST
    useful surface. Reporting server -> read-only queries,
    NOT a general "run SQL" or "run command" escape hatch.
    ROOTS (T22) enforce this on the file system.
    ----
    WHY USED : least privilege applied at the SERVER surface.
    SOLVES   : blast radius — expose the narrowest useful capability.
    EXAMPLE  : reporting server exposes read-only queries, NOT
               a general "run SQL" escape hatch.
    WHO CALLS: YOU (server author) decide what the server exposes.
  end note

  :T24 · PRODUCTION: AUTH · VERSIONING · RELIABILITY;
  note right
    * AUTH — remote servers use real auth (OAuth); SERVER enforces
      permissions, never trusts the client.
    * VERSIONING — protocol version (the language spoken) is
      negotiated at handshake; ALSO version YOUR tools —
      changing a schema breaks every client.
    * RELIABILITY — timeouts, sane retries, graceful degradation:
      if search server dies, agent SAYS so, doesn't hang.
    ----
    WHY USED : a server used by others must be safe + stable.
    SOLVES   : unauthorized access, breaking changes, silent hangs.
    EXAMPLE  : OAuth on the remote server; bump tool version when a
               schema changes so old clients don't break.
    WHO CALLS: SERVER enforces auth; YOU own versioning/reliability.
  end note
}

' ---------- TOPICS 25–26: BUILT-IN vs CUSTOM ----------
partition "T25–T26 · BUILT-IN vs CUSTOM" {
  :T25 · BUILT-IN toolset (ships with Claude);
  note right
    web search, code execution (sandboxed Python),
    computer use, text editor, memory tool.
    These are SERVER tools (D1): Anthropic executes,
    you write NO execution code.
    ----
    WHY USED : common capabilities you shouldn't rebuild.
    SOLVES   : maintenance — Anthropic runs + updates them.
    EXAMPLE  : web search, sandboxed code execution.
    WHO CALLS: MODEL invokes; ANTHROPIC executes server-side.
  end note

  if (Does a built-in already cover it?) then (YES)
    #LightGreen:T26 · USE the built-in (no maintenance, pre-tested);
  else (NO — touches YOUR systems)
    #LightGreen:T26 · Build CUSTOM (your DB / API / business logic);
  endif
  note right
    == T26 · BUILT-IN vs CUSTOM (the decision) ==
    WHY USED : choose the cheapest sufficient option.
    SOLVES   : needless custom code + ownership burden.
    EXAMPLE  : web/calc -> built-in; your CRM / private docs -> custom.
    WHO CALLS: YOU (architect) decide at design time.
    DEFAULT  : PREFER the built-in when one exists (no upkeep, tested,
               model already knows it). Go CUSTOM only when you need
               YOUR data, governance/audit, compliance, or a shaped
               output the built-in can't give.
    TRADE    : built-in = speed + zero maintenance;
               custom = control + specificity, paid for in ownership.
  end note
}

stop

floating note left
  == PART B EXAM TRAPS (memorize cold) ==
  1. MCP is NOT a new transport — it RUNS on HTTP.
     Its value = standardization + DISCOVERY (plug-and-play), model-agnostic.
  2. Claude REQUESTS; the CLIENT executes; the SERVER holds creds + hits backend.
  3. PRIMITIVE OWNERSHIP: TOOL=model, RESOURCE=app, PROMPT=user.
  4. RESOURCE = given as context (Claude can't pick it); TOOL = requested.
  5. STDIO = local, no auth; STREAMABLE HTTP = remote/prod, auth + progress.
  6. SAMPLING = server asks the model THROUGH the client (client keeps the key).
  7. PROTOCOL VERSION (spoken language) != TOOL VERSION (your schema); both matter.
  8. Built-in tools are SERVER tools — you run no code.
  ----
  Flow in one line: STANDARD INTERFACE -> connect+HANDSHAKE -> list-tools
  (DISCOVER) -> Claude ROUTES on descriptions -> CLIENT executes -> SERVER runs it.
end note

@enduml
